description: C# conventions for Sportland scripts — namespaces, runtime wiring, Unity idioms
globs: Assets/Scripts/**/*.cs,Assets/Editor/**/*.cs
alwaysApply: false
---

## Namespaces mirror folders

`Assets/Scripts/Sports/Dodgeball/Foo.cs` → `namespace Sportland.Sports.Dodgeball`.
No asmdefs exist, so everything shares one `Assembly-CSharp` — a compile error
anywhere takes the whole game down, and any type is reachable from any other.

Existing namespaces: `Sportland.Core`, `.Core.GameManagement`, `.Core.Athletes`,
`.Core.Calendar`, `.Core.Flags`, `.Core.Player`, `.Career`, `.Sports.Dodgeball`,
`.Sports.Demoball`, `.Sports.Basketball.Gameplay`, `.Sports.Basketball.Stats`,
`.Sports.Tag`, `.Sports.Shared`, `.Hub`, `.UI.Hub`, `.InputHandling`, `.Movement`,
`.Rendering`, `.Diagnostics`, `.World`.

## Wiring is mostly runtime, not scene-authored

`CourtSetup` (on `DodgeballField`) builds the court, spawns the 12 players, the ball,
the diagnostics HUD, and the debug cannon in `Start()`. Components it adds never pass
through the Inspector, so **their tuning comes from script field defaults**. To change
a tuned value, change the default in the script — don't write instructions to set it
in the Inspector, and don't add a serialized field expecting someone to populate it.

Adding a new runtime-spawned system means extending `CourtSetup` behind a bool flag,
the way `showDiagnosticsHud`, `spawnDebugCannon`, and `physicsDebugAssistant` work.

## Stats and ratings

- Read `Effective*` properties, never raw base attributes — that's the seam that makes
  Special Abilities and stamina apply everywhere without per-system code.
- Convert with `Rating.To01(v)`; display with `Rating.Grade(v)`. Don't reintroduce the
  legacy 0–100 attribute scale in new code.
- A rating maps to its in-sim effect through a **linear floor→ceiling** mapping
  (e.g. Throw Power → 12 u/s at 0, 36 u/s at 20). Elite differentiation is supposed to
  come from the abilities layer, not from curving a base stat.
- Abilities are on/off per player, compose multiplicatively, and clamp to [0,1].
  See `design/special_abilities.md` for the locked architecture before adding one.

## Unity idioms in this codebase

- 1 Unity unit = 1 metre; the dodgeball court is 18 × 9 with origin at centre.
- Ball physics decouple **lateral** motion (court plane, with drag) from a separate
  vertical `Height` with its own gravity sim. Don't collapse them into a 3D vector.
- Hit detection uses each player's vertical body band (`BodyBottom`/`BodyTop`), which
  jumping and ducking reshape.
- Input flows through `IInputSource` / `InputBroker`, with parallel player and AI
  sources — add behaviour there rather than reading devices directly in gameplay code.
- Dev-only tooling must be compiled out or self-destruct in release builds, the way
  `PhysicsDebugAssistant` does.
- Match the surrounding comment style: sparse, explaining *why* a model was chosen.
  Don't narrate what the code does.

## New files

A new `.cs` file needs a sibling `.cs.meta`. Generate it with
`node tools/unity-meta.mjs --fix`, and commit both.
